  {
    "_ulid": "01KJDQ1513YJA4PKRTA8CZDE4P",
    "slugs": [],
    "title": "kspec task set --priority accepts numbers (1-5) but all documentation, conventions, and plan documen",
    "type": "task",
    "description": "**What**: `kspec task set --priority` and `kspec task add --priority` only accept raw numbers (1-5), but all documentation, conventions, and plan documents use P1/P2/P3 notation. The error message says 'Priority must be a number between 1 and 5' which is confusing when P-notation is used everywhere else. **Where**: `src/cli/validators.ts` — the `parseIntOption()` function (line 36-64) is used for priority parsing. It calls `Number(value)` which rejects `P1` etc. The priority option is defined in `src/cli/commands/task.ts` at lines 608, 827. The `parseIntOption` is called at lines 287-296 (task set) and 759-767 (task add). **How**: Create a `parsePriority()` wrapper function in `src/cli/validators.ts` that first normalizes P-notation before calling `parseIntOption()`. Implementation: strip a leading case-insensitive `P` prefix if present (`value.replace(/^[Pp]/, '')`), then delegate to `parseIntOption()`. Replace all `parseIntOption(options.priority, { min: 1, max: 5, name: 'Priority' })` calls in `task.ts` with `parsePriority(options.priority)`. Update the option description from 'Priority (1-5)' to 'Priority (1-5 or P1-P5)'. Update the error message to mention both formats. **Done when**: (1) `kspec task set @ref --priority P1` works and sets priority to 1. (2) `kspec task add --priority p3` works (case-insensitive). (3) `kspec task set @ref --priority 2` still works (backward compatible). (4) Invalid values like `P6`, `Px`, `0` are rejected with a clear error mentioning both formats. (5) Tests in `tests/cli-validators.test.ts` cover both formats. (6) CLI help text shows accepted format.",
